## Scales and the dynamometer: the name, the description.
scale-kitchen = Kitchen scales (up to 5 kg)
scale-kitchen-scale-description-novice = Put a pot on them and the screen shows its mass in grams. They hold up to 5 kilograms.
scale-kitchen-scale-description-school = Electronic scales with a 1 g division: they do not notice smaller changes of mass. The "tare" button zeroes the reading to weigh only what is in the vessel.
scale-lab-balance = Laboratory balance (up to 620 g)
scale-lab-balance-description-novice = Precise laboratory scales: they show mass to a hundredth of a gram. But nothing heavy — they hold only 620 grams.
scale-lab-balance-description-school = The division is 0.01 g — a hundred times finer than kitchen scales: you can see a few tenths of a gram of water evaporate. The more precise the scales, the smaller their maximum load.
scale-dynamometer = Dynamometer (up to 5 N)
scale-dynamometer-description-novice = A spring with a scale and a hook. Hang a body on it — the spring stretches and the pointer shows how hard the body pulls down.
scale-dynamometer-description-school = Measures force in newtons (up to 5 N, division 0.1 N). A body in air pulls with P = m·g; under water less, by the buoyant force.
